================================================================================
RxNorm and RxNav (drug-name coding, herald/terminology/)
================================================================================

Herald codes medication and allergen names using RxNorm, produced by the
U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM), National Institutes of Health,
Department of Health and Human Services. Herald uses only the RxNorm
Current Prescribable Content subset, which is public domain and requires
no UMLS Metathesaurus License. The pinned release, its source URL and its
sha256 checksum are recorded in config/terminology.yaml
(scripts/build_rxnorm_index.py rebuilds the local index from it; the index
itself is not committed to this repository).

Brand names outside the prescribable subset (active-but-not-prescribable
and retired brand names) are supplemented from NLM's public RxNav REST API
(https://rxnav.nlm.nih.gov/REST), subject to the RxNav Terms of Service:
https://lhncbc.nlm.nih.gov/RxNav/TermsofService.html

NLM disclaimer: RxNorm and RxNav are provided by NLM "as is," and NLM makes
no warranties, express or implied, including no warranty of merchantability
or fitness for a particular purpose, regarding RxNorm, RxNav, or any
derivative work such as Herald's local drug-coding index. Herald is not
endorsed by, and is not a product of, the National Library of Medicine or
the National Institutes of Health.

Herald never distributes a full copy of RxNorm; it distributes only the
small index scripts/build_rxnorm_index.py builds from the public release,
and that index is not committed to this repository (each clone rebuilds
its own, per README.md).

================================================================================
Santa Clara County EMS Agency protocol documents (data/protocols/santa_clara/)
================================================================================

The PDFs under data/protocols/santa_clara/archive/ are unmodified copies of
public records published by the Santa Clara County Emergency Medical
Services Agency (protocols, policies and administrative orders governing
prehospital care in Santa Clara County, California), retrieved via public
web archives (web.archive.org) as noted in config/counties/santa_clara.json.
They are reproduced here exactly as published, under their original file
names, for reference by Herald's protocol-lookup feature; Herald does not
claim authorship of their content and always cites the document, section
and effective date it quotes from. These are government records; verify
any protocol text against the county's own in-force manual before clinical
use (config/counties/santa_clara.json's review note).

================================================================================
California POLST form (data/forms/, scripts/vision_train/)
================================================================================

The POLST (Physician Orders for Life-Sustaining Treatment) form referenced
by Herald's vision prompts and training renderer is the California POLST
form, EMSA #111 B, effective 4/1/2017 (English) and its 2016 Spanish
translation, published by the California POLST program
(https://capolst.org) in coordination with the California Emergency
Medical Services Authority. The form is designed for free reproduction and
distribution for its intended clinical and educational use; Herald
reproduces it unmodified for vision-model training and reads it only to
report which resuscitation box is checked (see MODEL_CARD.md and
"Claims" in README.md for exactly what Herald does and does not claim
about reading it). The California POLST form and program are not affiliated
with, and do not endorse, Herald.

================================================================================
Published clinical scores (data, not software; cited for provenance)
================================================================================

NEWS2 (Royal College of Physicians), RACE, G.F.A.S.T. (Santa Clara County
EMS Protocol 700-A13) and the 2021 National Guideline for the Field Triage
of Injured Patients are reproduced as published score definitions and
thresholds in config/scores/*.yaml, with their sources cited in README.md
under "Evidence behind the scores." Herald computes these scores in plain
code from confirmed facts; it does not modify the published definitions.
